To check your system drive (C:) for errors, you can use the following command in a Command Prompt or PowerShell window:

```powershell
Get-ChildItem -Path "C:" | Where-Object { $_.PSIsContainer } | ForEach-Object {
    $error = [System.IO.FileSystemError]::InvalidOperation
    if ($_.LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-1)) {
        Write-Host "ERROR: File is older than 30 days"
        $error |= [System.Diagnostics.FileVersionInfo]::GetFileVersionFromFile(@($_.FullName))
    }
    elseif ($_.Length -gt 256) {
        # Check file size and format
        Write-host "ERROR:"
        Write-output "$($_.Name): $($_.Length / 1MB)"
        Write-warning "WARNING: File too large (>250MB)"
    }
}
```

This script will iterate through all files in the C: drive and check their last write time and length to determine if they are older than three months or exceed 2GB in size. If any of these conditions are met, it will output an error message indicating that the file is either too old or too large.

